hi my name is ian russell i'm the  associate director of marketing at  dovetail genomics and  in this presentation i'm going to be  talking to you about expanding the hi-c  toolbox  and introduce you to some new  high-resolution approaches  that we have applied to diptone diploid  genome phasing  protein-directed chromatin architecture  mapping and more  in this presentation i'm going to give  you a brief introduction  into chromatin conformation capture just  so you're oriented  uh in terms of how that data is  generated and how to interpret it  and then i'm going to go into detail  about some of the new products that  we've launched and the applications  which you can use those for  there are a variety of methods for  chromatin conformation capture which are  depicted here  all of these methodologies share the  same core workflow that i'll walk you  through  on the left hand side what you can see  is a cross-linking step  this cross-linking step is designed to  cross-link  chromatin in situ and thereby capture  the 3-d conformation of the genome  in its natural state the next step is to  digest that crosslink chromatin  typically this is done using restriction  enzymes  in my cartoon here you can see that the  blue and the red molecules  which are found in close proximity may  actually be fairly distant  in linear space along the genome the  next  phase or step within the workflow is a  ligation  in which the pairs are ligated together  the cross link is reversed and the  output  is a chimeric molecule that can be  analyzed in a variety of different ways  the current methodology or the most  popular methodology today for doing this  is a process called high c in which the  readout of that chimeric molecule  is performed on the next gen generation  sequencing platform  in terms of interpreting the data what  one is  creating is a pair of reads or a read  pair  from the next gen sequencing library  that can then be mapped back  to the genome position if  those two pairs are  next to each other in linear sequence  space then they would fall  on the x-axis or that blue bar down at  the bottom  and the distance between the read pairs  in terms  of space separation it moves them  upwards on the y-axis so you can see in  my cartoon  here at the bottom there are three  different examples of how far a read  pair may  map on the genome position and where  that gets plotted within  what's called a contact matrix the  longest molecule or the longest distance  is up at the top and a  45 degree diagonal is drawn from the  position  of where one half of that repair maps  and where the other half of that read  pair maps to  a point that's up at the top the  intermediary  one which is depicted by the two dots  in the middle of the contact matrix  show an intermediary distance between  the two  parts of the chimeric molecule and then  there's an example of  a read pair where they are much closer  in proximity or in linear space to each  other  so this is the basic premise of a lot of  the data that i'm going to show you  later in the presentation  the way that it actually looks within  the data set  is not quite as clear as what i've  depicted there  and so here you're looking at the data  from two different chromosomes  typically there's very few read pairs  which are  resulting from ligations that are  interchromosomal  and you can see that it starts to build  up these triangular  shapes within the data that represent  regions  that are have a high probability of  being  in close proximity to each other and  i'll go into a little bit more detail  later in the presentation as to the  significance of that  what's clear is that one can use this  data to start to  uh arrange um the the order of fragments  uh through genome assembly um for  uh mapping purposes and i'll go into  that  in some subsequent slides here  now one unique approach at dovetail  genomics  is to move away from restriction enzymes  for that fragmentation process  the reason for doing this is that the  restriction enzyme approaches  are biased as depicted in these  graphs here what you're looking at with  the dotted  uh black line is the typical  distribution  of a shotgun library where the  target coverage is approximately 30x  you can see with restriction a single  restriction enzyme approach  you get a lot of bias  introduced into that data where a lot of  the genome is not covered very much at  all  and then there are regions which are  overly covered on the right hand side  by adding in additional enzymes for a  multi-restriction enzyme approach  you do address that to some extent but  you're still not approximating  what a shotgun library is capable of  and with the dovetail approach which  uses sequence independent endonucleases  we get very close to that shotgun  distribution which is  ideal both in terms of  coverage and the ability to use the data  in a multitude of ways  that i'll describe to you  secondly the dovetail approach provides  libraries of very high quality  there are three main ways of assessing  the quality  of a high c library the first one is the  number of valid repairs  that is the total percent total of the  library  that gives long range information  for the most part researchers are  interested in the cis read pairs and so  we look very closely at those  and what we're plotting here are three  different distances  distance buckets so reads that represent  interactions greater than  1kb in separation 20 kb  and 1 megabase and then lastly library  complexity  which is important in terms of  utilizing your sequence in the most  efficient fashion and as you can see  in the blue bars which depict a typical  dovetail library  we outperform both the multi-restriction  enzyme and the single restriction enzyme  approaches  so from this one can start to  apply high c to a variety  of different applications which prior  restriction enzyme approaches did not  support  so the detection and structural variant  sniff and indel genotyping genome  assembly and hassle type phasing  and lastly chromatin conformation and so  through the rest of my presentation  i'm going to introduce you to the  different products that we have  and how they can help you in terms of  addressing  those applications specifically the  first product i want to focus on is the  omni-c kit  this is a kit which is really being  designed to provide superior uniform  coverage and  mimics that shotgun library and it's  really the most flexible kit that  dovetail  offers the workflow is depicted on the  lower portion of the slide here  i'm just going to point out the major  differences from that core workflow that  i shared with you initially  the first one is in the cross-linking  step instead of a single class  cross-linker we use a dual-class linking  approach which helps to enrich in the  long-range interaction  in the second step the fragmentation uh  is performed  using a dna's one enzyme which is the  sequencing  independent endonuclease and then lastly  within the ligation step  we use a oligonucleotide bridge  depicted by the orange molecule there  that is biotinylated  and this is used to enrich for those  chimeric molecules of interest  as i showed you before the omni-c  approach does an excellent job in  eliminating that sequence bias that's  seen within the restriction enzyme  approaches depicted  in the top two tracks here so you can  see there are holes in the coverage  uh both with the single restriction  enzyme and the multi-restriction enzyme  approach  whereas omni-c provides that very even  coverage  uh that i was referring to and so the  net result of this  is to lower the sequencing costs  associated with a particular study  here we're modeling the coverage needed  to reach  30x over 80 percent of the genome  and with a typical shotgun library  that's somewhere in the range of about  41x  there is a slight tax with the omnic kit  one needs 51x in order to reach that  metric  but that represents a fairly minor  increase in the overall sequencing cost  with a major increase in the type  and amount of data that one is producing  in such an experiment  that can be uh hi-c has traditionally  been used for denover scaffolding  and you can see a typical approach here  where one uses a combination of  short-range  platforms such as illumina or pac bio  combined with the long-range information  that a hi-c library provides  in this particular example omni-c does a  very good job  or an equal job to standard hi-c  in terms of being able to reach across  those large gaps within a  particular assembly and enable the  ordering and orientation of those  fragments the major advantage to using  omni-c  in a scaffolding environment  is depicted on this slide  so what we see when we compare  omni-c snip discovery or snip genotyping  to shotgun  is a very similar sensitivity and  precision  and that is significantly above what one  is able to achieve with the restriction  enzyme hi-c approach  this is important because it opens up  the possibility  of phasing the data and here we show an  example  of running that data through a  pipeline called hapcut2 where  we actually exceed the number of  heterozygous snips phased  compared to shotgun have a very low  switch error rate  and can get to full 23 chromosomes  phased for a mammalian genome  or human genome and compared to a  restriction enzyme  approach where yes you can get to that  fully phased  assembly however the switch error rate  is four-fold higher  we've now applied this to the atlantic  bluefin tuna genome  and i'm not going to go into any detail  in terms of the data on this slide but  suffice it to say  that we were able to get  to 24 major scaffolds which is the  expected number of chromosomes for that  particular species  so this is working very well for us  switching gears just a little bit i did  want to share with you  the exploration of the data that we've  done around structural variants  here we're showing data for a human  breast cell line  as well as a control where we're looking  at specific  inversion deletion and translocation  events which we know exists within that  particular data set  and as you can see those are clearly  depicted or clearly detected within the  omni-c data  as shown by the  bow tie shapes that are at the lower  panel  of the slide here  lastly before i finish up on omni-c i  just wanted to point out the degree of  flexibility  that the assay provides we have  produced an ultra-low input protocol  which enables  going down to very small cell inputs  down to a thousand cells so that opens  up possibilities with small sample input  such as that coming from facts  experiments  and the ability to work with these  library types with  standard hybrid capture panels and so  here's some examples in terms of the idt  xgen lockdown the illumina true site  agilent sure select to name just a few  there  are directly compatible with the data  type that's coming out of the omni-c  library  now i'm going to switch gear uh into the  epigenetics world and talk about two new  products that were launched at the  beginning of this year the first one we  call the micro microc kit  and the second one which is a evolution  of that  is the high chip m a's kit  before i get into that let me just  quickly talk about epigenetics  specifically for those of you familiar  with epigenetics you're probably using  tools such as bisulfite sequencing chip  seek or ataxi  all of these look at the genome in a  very linear  sense so for bisulfide sequencing  which of the  bases are methylated  on a linear sequence for gypsy  which regions are bound by a particular  protein or for attack seek  which regions are open  and accessible to the the transposes  within that assay  what i hope i've started to convince you  with is genomes are not  organized in a straight line the human  genome is about three meters in length  and it gets  compacted down into a six uh  uh micron nucleus  and that is done in a very organized  fashion using  a series of uh hierarchical genome  conformation features which are depicted  here  at a high level we have chromosome  territories where the  different chromosomes are actually  isolated from each other within the  nucleus  when you break that down there are  features which are called  av compartments and these essentially  map to  compartments or regions of dna which are  active in transcription  also known as euchromatin or inactive  and highly compacted or heterochromatin  zooming in further within that there are  topologically associated domains and  these  typically cluster genes that are linked  in function  and then even finer than that are  loops which mediate transcription factor  interactions so promoter enhancer  interactions  and with each of these one is zooming  further and further  into the topology of the genome for a  standard hi-c  library to do this you start to hit  limits of the resolution  of that approach as well as to get down  to a 5kv  map as depicted on the right-hand side  there  that requires enormously deep sequencing  and so there's a significant cost  associated with that  so what we've tried to do with microc is  to address  those two challenges and the way we've  done that is to take the standard  uh omni-c kit which i just shared with  you  and swap out the enzyme for an enzyme  called micrococcular nuclease or mnase  this nuclease is sequence independent  it has an endonuclease activity as well  as an exonuclease activity  and the end result of digestion with  mnas  is that you get short fragments that map  to the nucleosome specifically  a major advantage of that as shown in  this particular slide here  is that we get a much improved signal to  noise  over standard restriction enzyme  approaches  so you can see going from a  multi-restriction enzyme approach to  microc  there's about fourfold increase  in the number of  um a point of evidence  uh within the microc data set and over a  single restriction enzyme approach  uh that's about 16 fold more so that  means  both the ability to discover more  as well as savings in terms of cost  from a sequencing standpoint in not  having to sequence as deeply  the other major advantage is  shown in the next slide here  where we have taken  a protein called ctcf which binds to dna  and defines a lot of the topology that i  described in that earlier slide  cpcf is  used to  structure the nucleosomes and what you  can see here is  for the high occupancy ctsc  sites we've mapped out the  sequence on plus or minus 1kb on either  side of that  and you can see in the data the mapping  of the nucleosomes directly here as  depicted  by the peaks and valleys so each of  those peaks within the blue  data set represents the position of a  nucleosome  where the chromatin or dna was protected  from the micrococcal nuclease  and the valleys are the linker sequence  between nucleosome  where the micrococcular nuclease has  digested it  and so with this you can start asking  questions about  nucleosome positioning within the  context of  various topological domains and features  the last part of my talk i'm going to  build upon  the microc workflow and introduce you to  the high chip  product high chip basically uses a  similar  chemistry for the fragmentation and  proximity ligation  but introduces an enrichment approach  using antibodies to enrich for sequences  bound by a specific protein of interest  so again we're cross-linking up front we  do our fragmentation  prior to ligation however we do a  chromatin immunoprecipitation  and then we go through the same steps  that i shared with you before  the data that one gets out of this is  shown  here where we're comparing the dovetail  high chip data for  the ctcf binding factor with  chip seek data and what you can see  first of all is that the data looks very  similar  in terms of where the ctsc  is binding to the genome as depicted  by the peaks in both the green and the  red  graphs there but the high chip  data contains long-range interaction  information  that is shown by the arcs below the  green line  the green graph and each of those arcs  represent  an interaction in three-dimensional  space between  different ctsc ctcf molecules  not only that we can zoom in to a  specific ctcf  binding site and what you can see is  that compared to the chipseek data  the positioning of the nucleosomes  within that data set  is extremely clear as depicted by  the the peaks and valleys as i shared  with in that previous slide  now this assay is very easy to get up  and  running in the lab we have a panel of  pre-validated antibodies that are  available for it  to get you going before i finish out i  just wanted to share  one perspective in terms of how one  might start  using these different products in trying  to understand various aspects of  gene expression here we're looking at an  omni-c data set where we've  mapped the different a and b  compartments as shown by  the orange and the red tracks there  labeled pc1  where they are positive above the line  is an a compartment where they're below  the line is a b  compartment what you can see comparing  brain to liver is  there are differences between the two  and those  map to specific changes in  methylation of the histones as well as  gene expression changes  so just to finish up here hopefully what  i've convinced you of  is there's a variety of different  dovetail kits  that are available all of which use  sequence independent  nuclear endonucleases to help  maximize the data that one is getting  from your studies to address  a variety of different applications  including structural variant detection  and intel genotyping genome assembly  apple type phasing  and genome conformation with that i'm  going to stop here thank you for your  attention  and would be very happy to address any  questions you might have

